                    'content' => "Chapter One: The Map That Moved\n\nAt the edge of the northern forest, Elian found a map that changed whenever the moon rose. Roads appeared in silver ink, leading toward places no living traveler remembered. In the corner, one small lantern was drawn without a flame.\n\nThe village elders called it the Last Lantern. Long ago, seven lanterns had guarded the roads between the valley and the stars. Six had gone dark. When the seventh failed, the winter would have no end.\n\nElian packed bread, a blue compass, and his grandmother's red scarf. He followed the moving map into the forest, where each tree whispered a different direction. At midnight, the map showed a bridge that did not exist. Elian stepped forward anyway.\n\nOn the other side waited a ruined tower and a fox made of starlight. The fox carried a tiny key in its teeth.\n\n'You are late,' it said.\n\n'I did not know I was coming,' Elian answered.\n\nThe fox looked toward the dark sky. 'That is why the lantern chose you.'\n\nTogether they climbed the tower. At the top, Elian discovered that the final lantern was not empty. Its flame was hidden inside his own blue compass, glowing whenever he chose a path for someone else instead of himself. He placed the compass in the lantern, and a warm golden road opened across the sky.\n\nThe winter ended before dawn. Elian returned home with the moving map, but the map no longer showed roads. It showed people: every traveler who needed a light, and every brave heart willing to carry one.",
