                ['slug' => 'mathematics-made-clear-student-guide'],
                [
                    'category' => 'study',
                    'title' => 'Mathematics Made Clear: A Student Guide',
                    'author' => 'The Library Study Team',
                    'description' => 'A friendly full guide to arithmetic, fractions, decimals, percentages, algebra, geometry, data, and problem-solving.',
                    'cover_url' => 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1509228468518-180dd4864904?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=80',
                    'illustrations' => [
                        ['url' => 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1635070041078-e363dbe005cb?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1000&q=80', 'caption' => 'Numbers become easier when they are organized.'],
                        ['url' => 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1596495578063-5f7c0f6e6a65?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1000&q=80', 'caption' => 'Shapes and patterns help us understand space.'],
                    ],
                    'content' => "Mathematics Made Clear\nA Student Guide\n\nHow to Use This Guide\n\nMathematics is the study of patterns, quantities, shapes, and relationships. Do not try to memorize every rule at once. Read one chapter, work through the example, then solve a similar problem without looking at the answer. Show your steps: clear steps make mistakes easier to find and correct.\n\nChapter 1: Number Sense\n\nWhole numbers begin at zero and continue forever: 0, 1, 2, 3, and so on. The value of a digit depends on its place. In 4,582, the 4 means four thousands, the 5 means five hundreds, the 8 means eight tens, and the 2 means two ones.\n\nWhen comparing numbers, compare the greatest place first. Since 7,204 has seven thousands and 6,999 has six thousands, 7,204 is greater. Rounding gives an estimate. To round 3,746 to the nearest hundred, look at the tens digit, 4. Because 4 is less than 5, the answer is 3,700.\n\nChapter 2: The Four Operations\n\nAddition combines amounts. Subtraction finds the difference. Multiplication represents equal groups, and division shares an amount into equal groups.\n\nExample: 24 x 3 means three groups of 24, so 24 + 24 + 24 = 72. The related division facts are 72 / 3 = 24 and 72 / 24 = 3.\n\nUse the order of operations for mixed calculations: parentheses first, multiplication and division from left to right, then addition and subtraction from left to right. For 6 + 4 x 2, multiply first: 6 + 8 = 14.\n\nChapter 3: Fractions\n\nA fraction describes equal parts of a whole. The numerator is the top number and the denominator is the bottom number. In 3/5, the whole is divided into five equal parts and three are selected.\n\nEquivalent fractions have the same value. Multiply or divide the numerator and denominator by the same non-zero number: 1/2 = 2/4 = 4/8. To add fractions with the same denominator, add the numerators: 2/7 + 3/7 = 5/7. For different denominators, find a common denominator first.\n\nTo multiply fractions, multiply across: 2/3 x 3/4 = 6/12 = 1/2. To divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal: 1/2 / 3/4 = 1/2 x 4/3 = 2/3.\n\nChapter 4: Decimals and Percentages\n\nDecimals use place value after the decimal point. In 0.375, the 3 is tenths, the 7 is hundredths, and the 5 is thousandths. Line up decimal points when adding or subtracting.\n\nA percentage means out of one hundred. Therefore 25% = 25/100 = 1/4 = 0.25. To find 20% of 60, convert 20% to 0.20 and calculate 0.20 x 60 = 12.\n\nA discount subtracts from an original price. A 15% discount on 80 is 0.15 x 80 = 12, so the sale price is 80 - 12 = 68.\n\nChapter 5: Ratios and Proportion\n\nA ratio compares quantities. If a recipe uses 2 cups of rice for 3 people, the ratio is 2:3. Equivalent ratios are made by multiplying both parts by the same number.\n\nA proportion says two ratios are equal. For x/5 = 6/15, cross-multiply: 15x = 30, so x = 2. Always check whether the answer makes sense in the original situation.\n\nChapter 6: Algebra\n\nA variable is a letter that represents an unknown number. An expression such as 3x + 4 has terms, a coefficient, and a constant. An equation includes an equals sign.\n\nTo solve x + 7 = 12, subtract 7 from both sides: x = 5. To solve 3x = 18, divide both sides by 3: x = 6. For 2x + 5 = 17, subtract 5 to get 2x = 12, then divide by 2 to get x = 6.\n\nThe distributive property says a(b + c) = ab + ac. Therefore 3(x + 4) = 3x + 12. Combine like terms only: 2x + 5x = 7x, but 2x and 5 are not like terms.\n\nChapter 7: Geometry\n\nA point shows a location. A line extends forever in both directions. An angle measures a turn. Right angles measure 90 degrees, straight angles measure 180 degrees, and a full turn measures 360 degrees.\n\nThe perimeter is the distance around a shape. The area is the space inside it. A rectangle with length 8 and width 3 has perimeter 2(8 + 3) = 22 units and area 8 x 3 = 24 square units.\n\nThe area of a triangle is one-half times base times height: A = 1/2bh. The area of a circle is pi times radius squared: A = pi r^2. The circumference is 2 pi r. Use 3.14 as an estimate for pi when an exact answer is not required.\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem applies to right triangles: a^2 + b^2 = c^2, where c is the longest side. If the shorter sides are 3 and 4, then c^2 = 9 + 16 = 25, so c = 5.\n\nChapter 8: Measurement and Data\n\nChoose units that match the measurement. Length may use millimeters, centimeters, meters, or kilometers. Mass may use grams or kilograms. Time may use seconds, minutes, or hours. Convert units before comparing them.\n\nThe mean is the total divided by the number of values. The median is the middle value after sorting. The mode is the value that appears most often. The range is the greatest value minus the least value.\n\nFor the data 2, 3, 3, 6, 11, the mean is 25/5 = 5, the median is 3, the mode is 3, and the range is 11 - 2 = 9.\n\nChapter 9: Problem-Solving Method\n\nFirst, understand the question. Circle important information and identify what must be found. Second, choose a strategy: draw a picture, make a table, look for a pattern, write an equation, or work backward. Third, solve carefully and show the calculation. Finally, check the answer with estimation and a sentence explaining what it means.\n\nExample: A library has 4 shelves with 18 books on each shelf. It lends 23 books. How many remain? There are 4 x 18 = 72 books at first. Then 72 - 23 = 49 books remain.\n\nPractice Questions\n\n1. Round 6,748 to the nearest hundred.\n2. Calculate 36 / 4 + 5.\n3. Simplify 6/8.\n4. Find 30% of 90.\n5. Solve 4x + 2 = 18.\n6. Find the area of a rectangle measuring 9 by 5.\n7. Find the mean of 4, 6, 8, and 10.\n8. A right triangle has shorter sides 5 and 12. Find the longest side.\n\nAnswer Key\n\n1. 6,700. 2. 14. 3. 3/4. 4. 27. 5. x = 4. 6. 45 square units. 7. 7. 8. 13.\n\nFinal Study Reminder\n\nBe patient with difficult problems. Read the question twice, write what you know, and solve one step at a time. A wrong answer is useful when it shows you which step needs a new strategy. Mathematics becomes stronger through practice, explanation, and checking your work.",
                    'source_label' => 'Original library study guide',
                ],
            );

            $studyGuides = [
                ['slug' => 'english-skills-student-guide', 'title' => 'English Skills: A Student Guide', 'author' => 'The Library Study Team', 'description' => 'A practical guide to grammar, vocabulary, reading comprehension, paragraphs, and confident writing.', 'content' => "English Skills\nA Student Guide\n\nChapter 1: Building a Sentence\n\nA complete sentence has a subject and a predicate and expresses a complete thought. The subject tells who or what the sentence is about. The predicate tells what the subject does or is.\n\nA simple sentence has one main idea: The students read. A compound sentence joins two complete ideas with a comma and a coordinating conjunction such as and, but, or so: The students read, and they discussed the story.\n\nChapter 2: Parts of Speech\n\nNouns name people, places, things, or ideas. Verbs show action or state. Adjectives describe nouns, while adverbs describe verbs or adjectives. Pronouns replace nouns, and prepositions show relationships such as in, beside, or under.\n\nChapter 3: Reading for Meaning\n\nBefore reading, predict what the text may be about from its title and headings. During reading, underline important evidence and write questions. After reading, summarize the main idea in your own words. A good inference combines a clue from the text with what you already know.\n\nChapter 4: Paragraph Writing\n\nA paragraph begins with a topic sentence. Supporting sentences explain the idea with reasons, facts, or examples. A closing sentence connects back to the main point. Use linking words such as first, however, because, and finally.\n\nPractice\n1. Write a compound sentence about studying. 2. Find the verb in: The careful reader checked the answer. 3. Write a one-sentence summary of your favorite story. 4. Create a paragraph explaining why libraries matter.\n\nAnswer Key\n2. checked. The other answers can vary, but each should be complete, clear, and supported by details."],
                ['slug' => 'science-discovery-student-guide', 'title' => 'Science Discovery: A Student Guide', 'author' => 'The Library Study Team', 'description' => 'A clear introduction to living things, matter, energy, forces, Earth, and scientific investigation.', 'content' => "Science Discovery\nA Student Guide\n\nChapter 1: Thinking Like a Scientist\n\nScientists ask questions, observe carefully, make predictions, test ideas, record evidence, and explain results. A fair test changes one variable while keeping other conditions the same. Repeating an investigation makes the evidence more reliable.\n\nChapter 2: Living Things\n\nLiving things need energy, respond to their surroundings, grow, reproduce, and remove waste. Plants use light energy to make food through photosynthesis. Animals get energy by eating plants or other animals. Cells are the basic units of life.\n\nChapter 3: Matter and Energy\n\nMatter has mass and takes up space. Solids keep their shape, liquids flow but keep a volume, and gases spread to fill a container. Heating and cooling can change matter from one state to another. Energy can be transferred from one object to another, but it is not created or destroyed.\n\nChapter 4: Forces and Earth\n\nA force is a push or pull. Gravity pulls objects toward Earth, while friction slows moving objects. Earth has a crust, mantle, and core. Weather describes daily conditions; climate describes patterns over a long time.\n\nPractice\n1. Name the steps of a fair investigation. 2. Why do plants need light? 3. Give one example of a force. 4. Explain the difference between weather and climate.\n\nAnswer Key\nAnswers should include question, prediction, test, evidence, and conclusion; light provides energy for photosynthesis; gravity or friction is a force; weather is short-term and climate is long-term."],
                ['slug' => 'history-thinking-student-guide', 'title' => 'History Thinking: A Student Guide', 'author' => 'The Library Study Team', 'description' => 'A guide to timelines, primary sources, historical evidence, cause and effect, and respectful historical thinking.', 'content' => "History Thinking\nA Student Guide\n\nChapter 1: Time and Change\n\nA timeline places events in chronological order. Decades contain ten years and centuries contain one hundred years. Historians compare what changed and what stayed the same between periods.\n\nChapter 2: Sources and Evidence\n\nA primary source was created during the time being studied, such as a diary, law, photograph, tool, map, or speech. A secondary source explains the past later, often using many primary sources. Ask who created a source, when, where, why, and what it leaves out.\n\nChapter 3: Cause and Effect\n\nA cause helps explain why an event happened. An effect is what happened afterward. Historical events often have several causes and effects. Avoid reducing a complex event to one simple reason.\n\nChapter 4: Perspective\n\nPeople experience the same event differently because of their roles, locations, beliefs, and opportunities. Good historical writing uses evidence, identifies perspective, and avoids judging the past without understanding its context.\n\nPractice\n1. Put three events in chronological order. 2. Is a diary a primary or secondary source? 3. Write one possible cause and effect for a school opening. 4. What question helps reveal perspective?\n\nAnswer Key\n1. Earliest to latest. 2. Primary source. 3 and 4 can vary if the reasoning is clear and supported."],
                ['slug' => 'computer-basics-student-guide', 'title' => 'Computer Basics: A Student Guide', 'author' => 'The Library Study Team', 'description' => 'A beginner-friendly guide to hardware, software, algorithms, data, online safety, and digital citizenship.', 'content' => "Computer Basics\nA Student Guide\n\nChapter 1: Hardware and Software\n\nHardware is the physical equipment you can touch, such as a keyboard, screen, processor, and storage drive. Software is the instructions that tell hardware what to do. An operating system manages files, applications, memory, and devices.\n\nChapter 2: Data and Networks\n\nData can be text, numbers, images, sound, or video. A file stores data with a name and format. A network connects devices so they can share information. The internet is a worldwide network, while a website is a collection of connected pages.\n\nChapter 3: Algorithms and Coding\n\nAn algorithm is an ordered list of steps for solving a problem. A program turns an algorithm into instructions a computer can follow. Sequence means steps happen in order. Selection uses a decision, and repetition runs steps more than once.\n\nChapter 4: Safety and Citizenship\n\nUse strong, unique passwords and never share private information with strangers. Check a message before clicking a link. Treat people respectfully online, credit sources, and remember that digital actions can have lasting effects.\n\nPractice\n1. Explain hardware and software. 2. What is an algorithm? 3. Give an example of selection. 4. Name two ways to protect an account.\n\nAnswer Key\n1. Hardware is physical equipment; software is instructions. 2. Ordered steps for solving a problem. 3. If it is raining, take an umbrella. 4. Strong unique passwords and careful link checking."],
