        Book::updateOrCreate(
            ['slug' => 'complete-junior-senior-high-study-guide'],
            [
                'category' => 'study',
                'title' => 'Complete Junior and Senior High School Study Guide',
                'author' => 'The Library Study Team',
                'description' => 'A broad school guide covering Junior High subjects, Senior High core subjects, and ICT strand topics.',
                'cover_url' => 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1524178232363-1fb2b075b655?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=80',
                'illustrations' => [
                    ['url' => 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1503676260728-1c00da094a0b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1000&q=80', 'caption' => 'A study plan turns many subjects into manageable steps.'],
                    ['url' => 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1531482615713-2afd69097998?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1000&q=80', 'caption' => 'Learning grows through questions, practice, and teamwork.'],
                ],
                'content' => "Complete Junior and Senior High School Study Guide\n\nHow to Use This Book\n\nThis guide is a map, not a replacement for your teacher or full textbook. Read the section for your subject, write a short summary, and practice one skill. Keep a notebook for questions you want to ask in class. Review difficult topics several times instead of trying to memorize everything in one sitting.\n\nPART ONE: JUNIOR HIGH SCHOOL\n\nChapter 1: Mathematics\n\nMathematics develops number sense, reasoning, and problem-solving. Review whole numbers, integers, fractions, decimals, percentages, ratios, algebra, geometry, probability, and data. Show each step. For an equation such as 3x + 5 = 20, subtract 5 from both sides, then divide by 3: x = 5. For geometry, remember that rectangle area is length times width, triangle area is one-half base times height, and a right triangle follows a squared plus b squared equals c squared.\n\nWhen solving a word problem, identify what is known, what is unknown, the operation or formula needed, and whether the final answer makes sense. Estimate first so you can notice unreasonable results.\n\nChapter 2: Science\n\nScience uses observation, questions, predictions, fair tests, evidence, and conclusions. Biology studies living things and cells. Chemistry studies matter, atoms, mixtures, and changes. Physics studies forces, motion, energy, light, and sound. Earth science studies rocks, weather, climate, oceans, and space.\n\nIn an investigation, change one independent variable, measure the dependent variable, and keep controls the same. Record observations honestly, even when the result does not match the prediction.\n\nChapter 3: English\n\nEnglish includes grammar, reading, writing, speaking, and listening. A complete sentence has a subject and predicate. Use punctuation to guide meaning and paragraphs to organize ideas. When reading, identify the main idea, supporting details, sequence, author purpose, point of view, and evidence.\n\nA strong essay has an introduction with a clear claim, body paragraphs with evidence and explanation, and a conclusion that returns to the main idea. Revise for organization, word choice, grammar, spelling, and clarity.\n\nChapter 4: Filipino\n\nFilipino develops communication, pagbasa, pagsulat, pakikinig, pagsasalita, and appreciation of literature and culture. Sa pagbasa, tukuyin ang pangunahing ideya, mga pantulong na detalye, pananaw, at mensahe ng teksto. Sa pagsulat, ayusin ang mga ideya, gumamit ng wastong balarila, at piliin ang angkop na salita para sa mambabasa.\n\nAng panitikan ay maaaring magpakita ng karanasan, pagpapahalaga, kasaysayan, at kultura. Basahin ang tauhan, tagpuan, banghay, simbolismo, at aral ng isang akda.\n\nChapter 5: Araling Panlipunan\n\nAraling Panlipunan studies history, geography, economics, government, culture, and citizenship. Use timelines to arrange events and ask how geography, resources, leadership, and decisions shaped communities. A primary source comes from the period being studied; a secondary source explains the past later. Compare sources and consider perspective before forming a conclusion.\n\nA responsible citizen respects rights, understands duties, checks information, participates peacefully, and considers how decisions affect the community.\n\nChapter 6: Edukasyon sa Pagpapakatao\n\nEdukasyon sa Pagpapakatao develops ethical reasoning, self-knowledge, responsibility, respect, empathy, and good decision-making. Before choosing an action, identify the facts, people affected, possible consequences, values involved, and fair alternatives. Integrity means doing what is right even when no one is watching.\n\nChapter 7: MAPEH\n\nMusic studies rhythm, melody, harmony, form, dynamics, instruments, and musical traditions. Practice listening for a steady beat and describe how music creates feeling.\n\nArts studies line, shape, color, texture, space, balance, contrast, emphasis, and cultural expression. Plan before creating, use materials safely, and explain the idea behind your work.\n\nPhysical Education develops fitness, movement skills, teamwork, and sportsmanship. Warm up, use correct technique, hydrate, rest when needed, and respect teammates and opponents.\n\nHealth covers nutrition, hygiene, mental health, safety, relationships, growth, disease prevention, and responsible choices. Reliable health information comes from qualified professionals and trusted public health sources.\n\nChapter 8: Technology and Livelihood Education / EPP\n\nTLE and EPP connect school learning to practical life and work. Topics may include agriculture, home economics, industrial arts, entrepreneurship, cooking, sewing, drafting, electronics, and safety. Follow instructions, measure carefully, care for tools, calculate cost, reduce waste, and evaluate the quality of a finished product.\n\nAn entrepreneur identifies a need, plans a useful product or service, estimates materials and cost, sets a fair price, and listens to customer feedback.\n\nChapter 9: Computer and ICT Foundations\n\nHardware is physical equipment; software is a set of instructions. Learn file management, word processing, spreadsheets, presentations, online research, digital communication, and basic troubleshooting. Protect accounts with unique passwords and never share private information carelessly.\n\nPART TWO: SENIOR HIGH SCHOOL CORE SUBJECTS\n\nChapter 10: Oral Communication\n\nEffective speaking considers purpose, audience, message, organization, voice, body language, and listening. A speech usually has an opening, clear points with evidence, transitions, and a conclusion. Respect different views and ask questions to understand before responding.\n\nChapter 11: Reading and Writing\n\nAcademic reading requires identifying claims, evidence, assumptions, tone, and bias. Academic writing uses a focused thesis, organized paragraphs, credible sources, quotations used carefully, and proper citation. Paraphrase in your own words and acknowledge the original source.\n\nChapter 12: General Mathematics\n\nSenior High mathematics extends algebra, functions, rational expressions, financial mathematics, logic, and business applications. A function connects each input to one output. When studying a graph, examine its domain, range, intercepts, rate of change, and pattern.\n\nChapter 13: Statistics and Probability\n\nStatistics collects and interprets data. Distinguish a population from a sample and avoid biased sampling. Mean, median, mode, range, variance, and standard deviation describe data. Probability measures how likely an event is, from 0 to 1. Independent events do not change one another's probability.\n\nChapter 14: Earth and Life Science\n\nEarth science examines systems such as the atmosphere, geosphere, hydrosphere, and biosphere. Life science studies cells, heredity, evolution, ecosystems, and biodiversity. Energy moves through food chains, while matter cycles through ecosystems. Protect habitats and use resources responsibly.\n\nChapter 15: Physical Science\n\nPhysical science joins chemistry and physics. Study atoms, elements, compounds, reactions, motion, force, work, energy, waves, electricity, and matter. Use units, diagrams, formulas, and measured evidence. A balanced chemical equation has the same number of each type of atom on both sides.\n\nChapter 16: Personal Development\n\nPersonal development involves self-awareness, identity, emotional regulation, relationships, goal-setting, and decision-making. Set specific goals, divide them into actions, track progress, and adjust when needed. Asking for help is a responsible strength, not a failure.\n\nChapter 17: Understanding Culture, Society and Politics\n\nCulture includes shared beliefs, practices, language, symbols, and values. Society is made of relationships and institutions. Politics concerns power, leadership, participation, policies, and how communities make decisions. Listen to multiple perspectives and evaluate claims using evidence.\n\nChapter 18: Media and Information Literacy\n\nMedia literacy means understanding how messages are created, distributed, and interpreted. Check the author, date, evidence, purpose, and other reliable sources before sharing information. Watch for misleading headlines, edited images, fake accounts, and emotional claims without proof.\n\nChapter 19: Physical Education and Health\n\nBuild fitness through regular activity, strength, flexibility, endurance, rest, hydration, and safe technique. Health decisions include nutrition, stress management, consent, healthy relationships, disease prevention, and knowing when to seek professional help.\n\nChapter 20: Introduction to Philosophy\n\nPhilosophy asks careful questions about knowledge, truth, reality, morality, beauty, and society. Define terms, state reasons, consider counterarguments, and distinguish a personal preference from an argument supported by reasons.\n\nChapter 21: Practical Research\n\nResearch begins with a focused question and a review of related information. Choose a method, gather ethical and relevant data, analyze results, report limitations, and cite sources. Never invent data or copy another person’s work.\n\nChapter 22: English for Academic and Professional Purposes\n\nAcademic and professional English includes reports, proposals, emails, summaries, resumes, instructions, and presentations. Use a respectful tone, precise vocabulary, logical organization, and evidence appropriate for the audience.\n\nPART THREE: ICT AND COMPUTER STRAND\n\nChapter 23: Computer Programming\n\nProgramming uses variables, data types, input, output, conditions, loops, functions, and testing. Break a large problem into small steps, write readable code, test normal and unusual cases, and explain how you fixed errors.\n\nChapter 24: Web Development\n\nHTML gives a page structure, CSS controls presentation, and JavaScript adds behavior. Use meaningful headings, accessible labels, responsive layouts, and secure handling of user input. Test on different screen sizes.\n\nChapter 25: Animation\n\nAnimation creates the appearance of movement through a sequence of images or poses. Study timing, spacing, anticipation, squash and stretch, follow-through, sound, storyboarding, and clear character action. Plan key frames before adding detail.\n\nChapter 26: Empowerment Technologies\n\nUse digital tools to create documents, media, presentations, online collaboration, and useful solutions. Choose tools based on purpose, audience, accessibility, privacy, and reliability.\n\nChapter 27: Computer Systems Servicing\n\nLearn safe installation, maintenance, troubleshooting, networking, and documentation. Turn off and unplug equipment when appropriate, protect components from static electricity, label cables, and keep a record of changes.\n\nChapter 28: Database Management\n\nA database organizes related data. Tables contain records and fields. A primary key identifies a record, while relationships connect tables. Use clear names, validation, backups, and appropriate permissions. Query only the data needed and protect private information.\n\nChapter 29: Work Immersion\n\nWork immersion connects school skills to a real workplace. Be punctual, respectful, prepared, teachable, and careful with confidential information. Keep a journal of tasks, feedback, skills practiced, and questions for improvement.\n\nStudy Plan\n\nChoose two subjects each week. For each subject, read one chapter, write five key words, answer practice questions, and explain the lesson aloud. At the end of the week, review mistakes and make a new plan.\n\nFinal Checklist\n\nCan you explain the main idea of each subject? Can you show your work in mathematics and science? Can you support claims with evidence? Can you communicate respectfully? Can you use technology safely and ethically? Learning is a process: ask, practice, check, improve, and try again.",
                'source_label' => 'Original library school study guide',
            ],
        );

