                ],
                'content' => "School Subjects: Examples and Practice\n\nHow to Study the Examples\n\nRead the question, identify the skill, follow the worked steps, and then solve the practice task without looking at the answer. Explain your reasoning in your own words.\n\nJUNIOR HIGH EXAMPLES\n\nMathematics\nExample: A shirt costs 800 pesos and is discounted by 25 percent. Find the discount and sale price.\nSolution: 25 percent of 800 is 0.25 x 800 = 200. Sale price = 800 - 200 = 600 pesos.\nPractice: Find 15 percent of 1,200. Answer: 180.\n\nScience\nExample: A plant is placed in darkness for several days. What process is affected?\nSolution: Photosynthesis is affected because plants need light to make food.\nPractice: Name the force that pulls objects toward Earth. Answer: Gravity.\n\nEnglish\nExample: Improve this sentence: The student was tired he finished the project.\nSolution: The student was tired, but he finished the project. The comma and conjunction join two complete ideas.\nPractice: Write a topic sentence about reading. Sample answer: Reading builds knowledge and imagination.\n\nFilipino\nHalimbawa: Tukuyin ang pangunahing ideya sa pangungusap: Mahalaga ang pagbabasa dahil pinapalawak nito ang kaalaman at bokabularyo.\nSagot: Ang pagbabasa ay nagpapalawak ng kaalaman at bokabularyo.\nPagsasanay: Sumulat ng isang pangungusap tungkol sa iyong layunin sa pag-aaral.\n\nAraling Panlipunan (AP)\nExample: Why should a historian compare two accounts of the same event?\nSolution: Different accounts may show different perspectives, evidence, or missing details. Comparing them creates a fairer conclusion.\nPractice: Is a diary written during an event a primary or secondary source? Answer: Primary source.\n\nEdukasyon sa Pagpapakatao (EsP)\nExample: You see a classmate being excluded from a group activity. What is a responsible response?\nSolution: Invite the classmate to participate, speak respectfully, and ask the group to follow a fair plan.\nPractice: Name one value shown by keeping a promise. Answer: Integrity or responsibility.\n\nMAPEH\nMusic example: A song becomes louder in the chorus. This change is called a dynamic change.\nArts example: Repeating a shape creates a pattern and can guide the viewer’s eye.\nPhysical Education example: Warm up before running to prepare muscles and reduce injury risk.\nHealth example: A balanced meal includes different food groups and enough water.\nPractice: Name one safe habit for exercise. Answer: Warm up, use correct form, hydrate, or rest when needed.\n\nTLE / EPP\nExample: A student plans to sell fruit cups. The materials cost 500 pesos and produce 25 cups. Cost per cup = 500 / 25 = 20 pesos. The selling price must cover the cost and allow a fair profit.\nPractice: List two safety rules when using tools. Answer: Use the correct tool and follow instructions; keep the work area clean.\n\nComputer / ICT\nExample: Create an algorithm for submitting homework: open the class portal, sign in, open the assignment, attach the file, check the file, and click submit.\nPractice: Is a keyboard hardware or software? Answer: Hardware.\n\nHomeroom / Values Education\nExample: A student has three projects due on Friday. They write a list, divide each project into smaller tasks, and schedule time each day. This shows responsibility and self-management.\n\nSENIOR HIGH CORE EXAMPLES\n\nOral Communication\nExample: A persuasive speech about clean water should state a claim, give evidence, explain why the evidence matters, and invite a practical action.\nPractice: Name one way to show active listening. Answer: Maintain attention, ask a clarifying question, or summarize the speaker’s point.\n\nReading and Writing\nExample: Claim: School gardens improve learning. Evidence: Students use the garden to observe plant growth and collect data. Explanation: The activity connects science concepts to direct experience.\n\nGeneral Mathematics\nExample: If f(x) = 2x + 3, then f(4) = 2(4) + 3 = 11.\nPractice: Solve 5x - 10 = 20. Answer: x = 6.\n\nStatistics and Probability\nExample: For 2, 4, 4, 6, and 9, the mean is 25 / 5 = 5, the median is 4, and the mode is 4.\nPractice: What is the probability of rolling a 6 on a fair six-sided die? Answer: 1/6.\n\nEarth and Life Science\nExample: In a food chain, grass is eaten by a grasshopper, which is eaten by a frog. Energy moves from the grass to the grasshopper and then to the frog.\n\nPhysical Science\nExample: If a car travels 100 meters in 20 seconds, speed = distance / time = 100 / 20 = 5 meters per second.\n\nPersonal Development\nExample: A SMART goal is: I will review biology for 20 minutes after dinner on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday for four weeks. It is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound.\n\nUnderstanding Culture, Society and Politics\nExample: Before sharing a claim about an election or community issue, compare reliable sources and consider whose voices are included or missing.\n\nMedia and Information Literacy\nExample: A post says a surprising fact but gives no author or source. Check the date, find the original source, compare trusted reports, and do not share it until verified.\n\nPhysical Education and Health\nExample: A safe weekly plan combines aerobic activity, strength, flexibility, rest, hydration, and appropriate nutrition. Increase difficulty gradually.\n\nIntroduction to Philosophy\nExample: An argument has a claim and reasons supporting it. Ask whether the reasons are relevant and whether the conclusion follows.\n\nPractical Research\nExample: Research question: Does study music affect concentration? Define concentration, select participants ethically, keep conditions consistent, record results, and state limitations.\n\nEnglish for Academic and Professional Purposes\nExample: Professional email structure: clear subject, respectful greeting, purpose in the first paragraph, specific request, thanks, and appropriate closing.\n\nICT STRAND EXAMPLES\n\nComputer Programming\nExample: A program can use a condition: if the score is 75 or higher, display Pass; otherwise, display Review. Test both outcomes.\n\nWeb Development\nExample: HTML creates a heading and paragraph, CSS changes their appearance, and JavaScript can respond when a button is clicked. Use labels and meaningful headings for accessibility.\n\nAnimation\nExample: To animate a bouncing ball, draw key poses at the highest and lowest points, add in-between frames, and use spacing to make the ball slow near the top and fast near the ground.\n\nEmpowerment Technologies\nExample: Choose a presentation tool based on audience, purpose, accessibility, privacy, and whether the audience can open the file.\n\nComputer Systems Servicing\nExample: When a computer will not start, check power, cables, display, error messages, and connections in a safe order. Record what you tested.\n\nDatabase Management\nExample: A student table can have student_id as the primary key, name as a field, and grade as another field. A query can find students with a grade above 90.\n\nWork Immersion\nExample: At the end of a workday, record the task completed, tool used, skill practiced, feedback received, and one goal for the next day.\n\nFINAL PRACTICE TASK\nChoose one subject. Write the question, show your steps, explain your answer, and list one thing you would check or improve. Learning is strongest when you can explain not only what the answer is, but why it works.",
                'source_label' => 'Original library examples guide',
            ],
        );

