                ['slug' => 'the-cherry-tree-panel', 'category' => 'manga', 'title' => 'The Cherry Tree Panel', 'author' => 'Mina Arai', 'description' => 'A complete school romance about two readers who meet in a flower-filled town and slowly become brave enough to speak.', 'opening' => "Chapter One: The Empty Seat\n\nYui always chose the library seat beside the window. From there she could see the shopping street, the old bookstore, and the cherry trees that turned the town pink every spring. She liked stories because characters knew what to say. Real conversations were much harder.\n\nOne afternoon, a boy stopped beside her table. He carried a book with a torn blue cover and looked as nervous as she felt.\n\n'Is this seat taken?' he asked.\n\nYui shook her head. 'No. You can sit here.'\n\nHis name was Haru. He read quietly, but every few pages he glanced at Yui's book. When she noticed, he turned red and pretended to study the ceiling. Before leaving, he placed a small cherry-shaped bookmark beside her hand.\n\n'It fell from my notebook,' he said. 'You can keep it if you want.'\n\nYui held the bookmark all the way home.", 'middle' => "Chapter Two: A Walk Through Spring\n\nThe next morning, Haru was waiting outside the library. He had two tickets for the flower market, but he had forgotten how to offer one. Yui solved the problem by pointing at the tickets and asking, 'Are those for today?'\n\nThey walked beneath branches full of pale blossoms. Haru told her about the hero in his book, a boy who could cross any ocean but could not say goodbye. Yui told him about her favorite heroine, a girl who collected small moments because she was afraid they would disappear.\n\nAt a cafe near the station, Yui bought strawberry ice cream. Haru tried to pay, but she gave him the cherry bookmark and said it was payment enough. Their classmates saw them sharing a table and began to whisper. Yui hid behind her cup. Haru laughed for the first time.\n\nLater, in the library, Haru returned her bookmark. On the back he had drawn the tree outside the window. Under it he had written, 'A good story should have a next chapter.'", 'ending' => "Chapter Three: The Next Chapter\n\nAt sunset, Yui and Haru climbed the hill above the town. The market lights came on below them, one by one, while petals moved across the path like tiny pages.\n\nYui opened the final page of her book. The heroine was standing at a crossroads, waiting for someone to speak first.\n\n'What do you think she should do?' Haru asked.\n\nYui looked at him. 'She should be honest.'\n\nHaru took a breath. 'Then I want to read the next volume with you. Tomorrow. And the day after that, if you are free.'\n\nYui smiled. 'I am free.'\n\nThey returned to the tree and sat together until the last light faded. They were still quiet, but the silence no longer felt empty. It felt like the space between panels, where a story continues even when no one is speaking.\n\nThe next morning, Yui found a new drawing inside her book: two readers beneath a cherry tree, with a blank speech bubble above them. She filled it in herself.\n\n'Let us begin.'", 'cover' => 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1578632767115-351597cf2477?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=80'],
